ach row \\(i\\) set  \n\n\\[\na_i=f(i)-1\\qquad(\\text{cells left of the uncovered one}),\\qquad\nb_i=n-f(i)\\qquad(\\text{cells right of the uncovered one}).\n\\]\n\nDefine the **left region**\n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le a_i\\},\n\\]\n\nand the **right region**\n\n\\[\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;f(i)+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery covered square belongs either to \\(L\\) or to \\(R\\); a tile must lie completely inside \\(L\\) or completely inside \\(R\\), because a tile cannot contain any point of \\(U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the number of tiles  \n\n##### 2.1  Left region  \n\nFor each row with \\(a_i>0\\) (i.e. \\(f(i)>1\\)) consider the right‑most cell of its left part:\n\n\\[\np_i=(i,a_i)=(i,f(i)-1).\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nP=\\{p_i\\mid f(i)>1\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(f\\) is a bijection, the columns \\(f(i)-1\\) are distinct; consequently \\(|P|=n-1\\), and the points of \\(P\\) lie in distinct rows and distinct columns.\n\n**Claim.** No axis‑aligned rectangle that avoids all uncovered squares can contain two distinct points of \\(P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake two rows \\(i<j\\) and their points \\(p_i,p_j\\).  \nThe rectangle determined by them has row interval \\([i,j]\\) and column interval  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl[\\min\\{f(i),f(j)\\}-1,\\;\\max\\{f(i),f(j)\\}-1\\bigr].\n\\]\n\nIf \\(f(i)<f(j)\\) then column \\(f(i)\\) lies inside this interval; the cell \\((i,f(i))\\) (the uncovered square of row \\(i\\)) is therefore inside the rectangle, contradicting the requirement that a tile may not cover an uncovered square.  \n\nIf \\(f(i)>f(j)\\) then column \\(f(j)\\) lies inside the interval, and the cell \\((j,f(j))\\) (the uncovered square of row \\(j\\)) is inside the rectangle, again a contradiction. ∎\n\nHence each tile that lies inside \\(L\\) can contain **at most one** point of \\(P\\).  \nBecause \\(|P|=n-1\\), at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are necessary to cover the whole left region.\n\n##### 2.2  Right region  \n\nFor each row with \\(b_i>0\\) (i.e. \\(f(i)<n\\)) consider the left‑most cell of its right part:\n\n\\[\nq_i=(i,f(i)+1).\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nQ=\\{q_i\\mid f(i)<n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich also has cardinality \\(n-1\\) and consists of points in distinct rows and distinct columns.  \nAn argument identical to the one above (now using the set \\(Q\\) and the right region) shows that any tile contained in \\(R\\) can contain at most one point of \\(Q\\).  \nConsequently at least another \\(n-1\\) tiles are required for the right region.\n\n##### 2.3  Combined lower bound  \n\nTherefore any admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(f(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).  \nThen \\(a_i=i-1\\) and \\(b_i=n-i\\).\n\n**Left tiles.**  \nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_j=\\{(i,k)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le k\\le j\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n\\(L_j\\) is a rectangle of size \\((n-j)\\times j\\).  \nIf \\((i,k)\\in L\\) then \\(k\\le i-1\\); setting \\(j=k\\) gives \\(i\\ge k+1=j+1\\), so \\((i,k)\\in L_j\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{L_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\) partitions the entire left region, and the rectangles are pairwise disjoint because they have different column ranges.\n\n**Right tiles.**  \nFor each \\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_j=\\{(i,k)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1,\\;j\\le k\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n\\(R_j\\) is a rectangle of size \\((j-1)\\times (n-j+1)\\).  \nIf \\((i,k)\\in R\\) then \\(k>i\\); setting \\(j=k\\) gives \\(i\\le k-1=j-1\\), so \\((i,k)\\in R_j\\).  \nHence the family \\(\\{R_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}\\) partitions the entire right region, and the rectangles are disjoint because they have different column ranges.\n\nThe two families are also disjoint from each other: every \\(L_j\\) uses only columns \\(\\le j\\), while every \\(R_{j'}\\) uses only columns \\(\\ge j'\\); for any pair we have either \\(j<j'\\) or \\(j\\ge j'\\), so the column intervals do not intersect.\n\nAll off‑diagonal squares are covered exactly once, while the diagonal squares remain uncovered, giving precisely one uncovered square in each row and each column.\n\nThe total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Minimality  \n\nInequality (2) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and construction (3)–(5) achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nHence the lower bound is tight, and the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the specific board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Answer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
