ly 14 tiles, and the lower‑bound argument shows that fewer tiles are impossible. Hence the minimum is 14.  \n\n\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nThe grid consists of the unit squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\).  \nMatilda must leave exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column.  \nConsequently the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: after a suitable permutation of the columns we may assume that the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus every square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\) has to be covered by a tile, and no tile may contain a square of \\(U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.   Rectangles as bipartite subgraphs  \n\nA tile is an axis‑aligned rectangle; it can be described by a pair of subsets  \n\n\\[\n(R,C),\\qquad R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwith the property  \n\n\\[\nR\\cap C=\\varnothing .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe tile covers exactly the squares  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in R,\\;j\\in C\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nHence a family \\(\\{(R_k,C_k)\\}_{k=1}^{m}\\) of such pairs partitions the set  \n\n\\[\nE:=\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\neq j\\}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\niff  \n\n\\[\nE=\\bigsqcup_{k=1}^{m}\\bigl(R_k\\times C_k\\bigr)\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n(the union is disjoint).  \nOur aim is to minimise \\(m\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.   A necessary antichain condition  \n\nFor each row index \\(i\\) define its *incidence vector*  \n\n\\[\nv_i=(\\mathbf 1_{i\\in R_1},\\mathbf 1_{i\\in R_2},\\dots ,\\mathbf 1_{i\\in R_m})\\in\\{0,1\\}^m .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nConsider two distinct indices \\(i\\neq j\\).  \nSince \\((i,j)\\in E\\), there exists a \\(k\\) with \\(i\\in R_k\\) and \\(j\\notin R_k\\); equivalently  \n\n\\[\nv_i(k)=1,\\qquad v_j(k)=0 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus for any ordered pair \\((i,j)\\) there is a coordinate where the vector of \\(i\\) has a 1 and that of \\(j\\) has a 0.  \nConsequently **no vector is component‑wise \\(\\le\\) another**; the set \\(\\{v_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\) is an *antichain* in the Boolean lattice \\(\\{0,1\\}^m\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.   Lower bound from Sperner’s theorem  \n\nSperner’s theorem states that the largest antichain in \\(\\{0,1\\}^m\\) has size  \n\n\\[\n\\max_{0\\le r\\le m}\\binom{m}{r}=\\binom{m}{\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nTherefore an antichain of size \\(n\\) can exist only if  \n\n\\[\nn\\le\\binom{m}{\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) we compute  \n\n\\[\n\\binom{13}{6}=1716<2025,\\qquad\n\\binom{14}{7}=3432>2025 .\n\\]\n\nHence (8) forces  \n\n\\[\nm\\ge 14 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus **any tiling satisfying the condition needs at least 14 tiles**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.   A tiling with 14 tiles  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{1,2,\\dots ,14\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\binom{14}{7}=3432>2025\\), we can select \\(2025\\) distinct subsets  \n\n\\[\nA_1,\\dots ,A_{2025}\\subseteq S,\\qquad |A_i|=7\\ \\text{for all }i .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n(Any such selection suffices; existence follows from the counting above.)\n\nFor each symbol \\(t\\in S\\) define a rectangle  \n\n\\[\n(R_t,C_t)\\quad\\text{by}\\quad\nR_t=\\{i\\mid t\\in A_i\\},\\qquad\nC_t=\\{j\\mid t\\notin A_j\\}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n*Because* \\(t\\) cannot simultaneously belong and not belong to the same set, (1) holds:\n\\(R_t\\cap C_t=\\varnothing\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.1   Every off‑diagonal square is covered  \n\nTake any ordered pair \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).  \nSince the subsets \\(A_i\\) and \\(A_j\\) are distinct and have the same size, the set difference \\(A_i\\setminus A_j\\) is non‑empty.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nt_0=\\min\\bigl(A_i\\setminus A_j\\bigr)\n\\tag{12}\