he vector of \\(i\\) has a 1 and that of \\(j\\) has a 0.  \nConsequently **no vector is component‑wise \\(\\le\\) another**; the set \\(\\{v_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\) is an *antichain* in the Boolean lattice \\(\\{0,1\\}^m\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.   Lower bound from Sperner’s theorem  \n\nSperner’s theorem states that the largest antichain in \\(\\{0,1\\}^m\\) has size  \n\n\\[\n\\max_{0\\le r\\le m}\\binom{m}{r}=\\binom{m}{\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nTherefore an antichain of size \\(n\\) can exist only if  \n\n\\[\nn\\le\\binom{m}{\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) we compute  \n\n\\[\n\\binom{13}{6}=1716<2025,\\qquad\n\\binom{14}{7}=3432>2025 .\n\\]\n\nHence (8) forces  \n\n\\[\nm\\ge 14 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus **any tiling satisfying the condition needs at least 14 tiles**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.   A tiling with 14 tiles  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{1,2,\\dots ,14\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\binom{14}{7}=3432>2025\\), we can select \\(2025\\) distinct subsets  \n\n\\[\nA_1,\\dots ,A_{2025}\\subseteq S,\\qquad |A_i|=7\\ \\text{for all }i .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n(Any such selection suffices; existence follows from the counting above.)\n\nFor each symbol \\(t\\in S\\) define a rectangle  \n\n\\[\n(R_t,C_t)\\quad\\text{by}\\quad\nR_t=\\{i\\mid t\\in A_i\\},\\qquad\nC_t=\\{j\\mid t\\notin A_j\\}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n*Because* \\(t\\) cannot simultaneously belong and not belong to the same set, (1) holds:\n\\(R_t\\cap C_t=\\varnothing\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.1   Every off‑diagonal square is covered  \n\nTake any ordered pair \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).  \nSince the subsets \\(A_i\\) and \\(A_j\\) are distinct and have the same size, the set difference \\(A_i\\setminus A_j\\) is non‑empty.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nt_0=\\min\\bigl(A_i\\setminus A_j\\bigr)\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nwith respect to the natural order on \\(S\\).  \nThen \\(t_0\\in A_i\\) and \\(t_0\\notin A_j\\); consequently \\(i\\in R_{t_0}\\) and \\(j\\in C_{t_0}\\), so \\((i,j)\\in R_{t_0}\\times C_{t_0}\\).\n\nIf \\(t<t_0\\) then either \\(t\\notin A_i\\) (so \\(i\\notin R_t\\)) or \\(t\\in A_j\\) (so \\(j\\notin C_t\\)).  \nHence \\((i,j)\\) does **not** belong to any rectangle \\((R_t,C_t)\\) with \\(t<t_0\\).  \nThus \\((i,j)\\) is contained in exactly one rectangle, namely the one indexed by \\(t_0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.2   No diagonal square is covered  \n\nA diagonal square \\((i,i)\\) would require a symbol \\(t\\) with simultaneously \\(t\\in A_i\\) and \\(t\\notin A_i\\), impossible.  \nHence every rectangle avoids all diagonal cells, i.e. the squares \\((i,i)\\) remain uncovered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.3   Resulting tiling  \n\nThe 14 rectangles \\(\\{(R_t,C_t)\\mid t\\in S\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover all squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).  \nConsequently they form a tiling of the grid with exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column (the diagonal squares).  \n\nThus a tiling using **14** tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.   Minimality  \n\nFrom the lower bound (9) we know that fewer than 14 tiles are impossible; the construction of §4 shows that 14 tiles are sufficient.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly 14.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\[\n\\boxed{14}\n\\]"
