r of critical cells is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{2}_{\\text{rows with }p(i)=1\\text{ or }n}\n\\;+\\;(n-2)\\cdot 2 \\;=\\;2n-2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A tile can contain at most one critical cell  \n\nLet \\(T\\) be a tile, i.e. a set of the form  \n\n\\[\nT=\\{(i,j)\\mid a\\le i\\le b,\\;c\\le j\\le d\\}\n\\]\n\nwith integers \\(1\\le a\\le b\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le c\\le d\\le n\\).  \nAssume that \\(T\\) contains two distinct critical cells.  \nWe show that then \\(T\\) must also contain a hole, contradicting the definition of a tile.\n\n*Case 1 – two right‑most‑left cells.*  \nSuppose \\(T\\) contains \\(L_{i_1}=(i_1,c_1)\\) and \\(L_{i_2}=(i_2,c_2)\\) with \\(c_1<c_2\\).  \nSince \\(c_2\\) is the column of the second critical cell, the column interval of \\(T\\) satisfies \\(c\\le c_1\\) and \\(d\\ge c_2\\).  \nConsequently column \\(c_1+1\\) lies inside \\([c,d]\\).  \nBut column \\(c_1+1\\) is precisely the hole of row \\(i_1\\) (because \\(p(i_1)=c_1+1\\)).  \nThus \\((i_1,c_1+1)\\in T\\), a hole – impossible.\n\n*Case 2 – two left‑most‑right cells.*  \nNow let \\(T\\) contain \\(R_{i_1}=(i_1,c_1)\\) and \\(R_{i_2}=(i_2,c_2)\\) with \\(c_1<c_2\\).  \nSince \\(c_1\\) is the leftmost column of \\(T\\) and \\(c_2\\) the rightmost, the column interval contains \\(c_2-1\\).  \nBut column \\(c_2-1\\) is the hole of row \\(i_2\\) (because \\(p(i_2)=c_2-1\\)).  \nHence \\((i_2,c_2-1)\\in T\\), again a hole – contradiction.\n\n*Case 3 – one right‑most‑left cell and one left‑most‑right cell.*  \nAssume \\(T\\) contains \\(L_{i_1}=(i_1,c_1)\\) and \\(R_{i_2}=(i_2,c_2)\\).  \nIf \\(c_1<c_2\\) then column \\(c_1+1\\) (the hole of row \\(i_1\\)) lies in \\([c,d]\\);  \nif \\(c_2<c_1\\) then column \\(c_2-1\\) (the hole of row \\(i_2\\)) lies in \\([c,d]\\).  \nIn either sub‑case a hole belongs to \\(T\\), a contradiction.\n\nThus **no tile can contain two distinct critical cells**. ∎\n\n-------------------