---------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nEvery tile covers at most one critical cell, while (1) shows that there are exactly \\(2n-2\\) critical cells.  \nTherefore any tiling of the board must use at least  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2n-2}\n\\]\n\ntiles.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor each column \\(j\\) (\\(1\\le j\\le n\\)) let  \n\n\\[\nr_j=\\text{the unique row with }p(r_j)=j .\n\\]\n\nThe column \\(j\\) contains all covered cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq r_j\\).  \nThese cells split naturally into two vertical intervals:\n\n* **Upper interval**  \n  \\[\n  U_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le r_j-1\\}\\quad\\text{(empty if }r_j=1\\text{)} .\n  \\]\n\n* **Lower interval**  \n  \\[\n  L_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid r_j+1\\le i\\le n\\}\\quad\\text{(empty if }r_j=n\\text{)} .\n  \\]\n\nBoth \\(U_j\\) and \\(L_j\\) are (possibly empty) rectangles of width 1 and height equal to the number of rows they contain.  \nPlace a tile on each non‑empty interval.  \n\n*If \\(r_j=1\\)* (the hole of column \\(j\\) lies in the first row) only the lower interval \\(L_j\\) is non‑empty, so we place one tile.  \n*If \\(r_j=n\\)* only the upper interval \\(U_j\\) is non‑empty, again one tile.  \n*For all other columns* both intervals are non‑empty, giving two tiles.\n\nSince exactly two columns contain the holes of the first and the last rows (namely the columns \\(p(1)\\) and \\(p(n)\\)), the total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n2n\\;-\\;2\\;=\\;2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThese tiles are pairwise disjoint, they avoid all holes by construction, and together they cover every cell of \\(C\\).  \nConsequently each row and each column has precisely one uncovered square, namely the hole belonging to that row or column.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Minimality  \n\nThe lower bound of Section 4 shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the construction of Section 5 achieves exactly this number.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Application to the original problem  \n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
