"### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:** I have found a complete solution.  \n**The final answer is** \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Permutation of uncovered squares:**  \n   The uncovered squares must form a permutation matrix; write the uncovered cell in row\u202f\\(i\\) as \\((i,p(i))\\) where \\(p\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(n=2025\\).\n\n2. **Critical cells:**  \n   For each row \\(i\\) define the *rightmost* covered cell left of the hole, \\((i,p(i)-1)\\) (if \\(p(i)>1\\)), and the *leftmost* covered cell right of the hole, \\((i,p(i)+1)\\) (if \\(p(i)<n\\)).  \n   These are called *critical cells*. There are exactly \\(2n-2\\) of them (every row contributes two except the two rows whose holes lie on the extreme columns, which contribute one each).\n\n3. **Upper bound on a tile\u2019s critical cells:**  \n   Prove that any axis\u2011aligned rectangle (tile) can contain **at most one** critical cell.  \n   The proof examines the three possible ways two distinct critical cells could lie in the same rectangle and shows that in each case the rectangle would necessarily contain a hole, contradicting the rules.\n\n4. **Lower bound:**  \n   Since each tile can cover at most one critical cell and there are \\(2n-2\\) critical cells, any tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n5. **Construction attaining the bound:**  \n   For each column \\(j\\) let \\(r_j\\) be the unique row with the hole in that column (\\(p(r_j)=j\\)).  \n   - Place a vertical tile covering the cells \\((1,j),\\dots,(r_j-1,j)\\) (if \\(r_j>1\\)).  \n   - Place a vertical tile covering the cells \\((r_j+1,j),\\dots,(n,j)\\) (if \\(r_j<n\\)).  \n   This yields exactly two tiles per column, except for the two columns whose holes are in the first or last row, where only one tile is needed. Hence the total number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   The tiles are disjoint, avoid all holes, and together cover every other unit square, so the required condition on rows and columns is satisfied.\n\n6. **Conclusion:**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction both give the same number, proving that the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nWe work with an \\(n\\times n\\) board whose unit squares are indexed by ordered pairs \\((i,j)\\) with  \n\\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nEach row must contain exactly one uncovered square, and each column must also contain exactly one uncovered square.  \nConsequently the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation  \n\\(p\\colon\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).  \n\nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nthe set of uncovered squares, and by  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\neq p(i)\\}\n\\]\n\nthe set of squares that must be covered by tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Critical cells  \n\nFor a row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n(i,p(i)-1), &\\text{if }p(i)>1,\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{undefined}, &\\text{if }p(i)=1,\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\nR_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n(i,p(i)+1), &\\text{if }p(i)<n,\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{undefined}, &\\text{if }p(i)=n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n\\(L_i\\) is the **rightmost** covered cell to the left of the hole in row \\(i\\);  \n\\(R_i\\) is the **leftmost** covered cell to the right of the hole in row \\(i\\).  \nWe call every defined \\(L_i\\) or \\(R_i\\) a **critical cell**.\n\nBecause \\(p\\) is a permutation, exactly one row has \\(p(i)=1\\) and exactly one row has \\(p(i)=n\\).  \nThese two rows contribute only one critical cell each, while every other row contributes two.  \nHence the total number of critical cells is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{2}_{\\text{rows with }p(i)=1\\text{ or }n}\n\\;+\\;(n-2)\\cdot 2 \\;=\\;2n-2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A tile can contain at most one critical cell  \n\nLet \\(T\\) be a tile, i.e. a set of the form  \n\n\\[\nT=\\{(i,j)\\mid a\\le i\\le b,\\;c\\le j\\le d\\}\n\\]\n\nwith integers \\(1\\le a\\le b\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le c\\le d\\le n\\).  \nAssume that \\(T\\) contains two distinct critical cells.  \nWe show that then \\(T\\) must also contain a hole, contradicting the definition of a tile.\n\n*Case 1 \u2013 two left\u2011neighbors \\(L_{i_1},L_{i_2}\\).*  \nSuppose \\(T\\) contains \\(L_{i_1}=(i_1,c_1)\\) and \\(L_{i_2}=(i_2,c_2)\\) with \\(c_1<c_2\\).  \nSince both cells lie in \\(T\\), we have \\(c\\le c_1\\) and \\(d\\ge c_2\\); consequently column \\(c_1+1\\) lies in \\([c,d]\\).  \nBut column \\(c_1+1\\) is precisely the hole of row \\(i_1\\) (because \\(p(i_1)=c_1+1\\)).  \nThus \\((i_1,c_1+1)\\in T\\), a hole \u2013 impossible.\n\n*Case 2 \u2013 two right\u2011neighbors \\(R_{i_1},R_{i_2}\\).*  \nNow let \\(T\\) contain \\(R_{i_1}=(i_1,c_1)\\) and \\(R_{i_2}=(i_2,c_2)\\) with \\(c_1<c_2\\).  \nSince \\(c\\le c_1\\) and \\(d\\ge c_2\\), column \\(c_2-1\\) belongs to \\([c,d]\\).  \nColumn \\(c_2-1\\) is the hole of row \\(i_2\\) (because \\(p(i_2)=c_2-1\\)).  \nHence \\((i_2,c_2-1)\\in T\\), again a hole \u2013 contradiction.\n\n*Case 3 \u2013 one left\u2011neighbor and one right\u2011neighbor.*  \nAssume \\(T\\) contains \\(L_{i_1}=(i_1,c_1)\\) and \\(R_{i_2}=(i_2,c_2)\\).  \nIf \\(c_1<c_2\\) then column \\(c_1+1\\) (the hole of row \\(i_1\\)) lies in \\([c,d]\\);  \nif \\(c_2<c_1\\) then column \\(c_2-1\\) (the hole of row \\(i_2\\)) lies in \\([c,d]\\).  \nIn either sub\u2011case a hole belongs to \\(T\\), a contradiction.\n\nThus **no tile can contain two distinct critical cells**. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nEvery tile covers at most one critical cell, while (1) shows that there are exactly \\(2n-2\\) critical cells.  \nTherefore any tiling of the board must use at least  \n\n\\[\n2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor each column \\(j\\) (\\(1\\le j\\le n\\)) let  \n\n\\[\nr_j=\\text{the unique row with }p(r_j)=j .\n\\]\n\nThe column \\(j\\) contains all covered cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq r_j\\).  \nThese cells split naturally into two vertical intervals:\n\n* **Upper interval**  \n  \\[\n  U_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le r_j-1\\}\\quad\\text{(empty if }r_j=1\\text{)} .\n  \\]\n\n* **Lower interval**  \n  \\[\n  L_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid r_j+1\\le i\\le n\\}\\quad\\text{(empty if }r_j=n\\text{)} .\n  \\]\n\nBoth \\(U_j\\) and \\(L_j\\) are (possibly empty) rectangles of width\u202f1.  \nPlace a tile on each non\u2011empty interval.\n\nIf \\(r_j=1\\) (the hole of column \\(j\\) lies in the first row) only the lower interval \\(L_j\\) is non\u2011empty, so we place one tile.  \nIf \\(r_j=n\\) only the upper interval \\(U_j\\) is non\u2011empty, again one tile.  \nFor all other columns both intervals are non\u2011empty, giving two tiles.\n\nExactly two columns contain the holes of the first and the last rows (namely the columns \\(p(1)\\) and \\(p(n)\\)), so the total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n2n\\;-\\;2\\;=\\;2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThese tiles are pairwise disjoint, they avoid all holes by construction, and together they cover every cell of \\(C\\).  \nHence each row and each column has precisely one uncovered square, namely the hole belonging to that row or column.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Minimality  \n\nThe lower bound of Section\u202f4 shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the construction of Section\u202f5 achieves exactly this number.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Application to the original problem  \n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
