old because a rectangle could cover cells from multiple columns that are present in the same rows. However, the lemma's proof incorrectly assumes columns are contiguous (no gaps). So the lower bound may be too high. But maybe the shape L has the property that each column's cells form a contiguous block of rows (i.e., there are no gaps). Let's examine: For a given column j, the set of rows i where c_i > j is exactly the set of rows where the uncovered column index c_i is greater than j. Since c_i runs through all numbers 1,...,n, the set of rows where c_i > j is exactly the rows where the uncovered column is among {j+1,...,n}. This set of rows may not be contiguous in the original ordering. For example, consider n=4 and permutation c = (2,4,1,3). Then c_i > 2 for rows i = 2 (c_2=4) and i=4 (c_4=3). So column j=2 has cells in rows 2 and 4, but not row 3. So column 2 has a gap. So column heights are not contiguous. So shape L is not a Young diagram; columns have gaps.\n\nThus the lower bound argument that each column must be covered by at least one rectangle may be false because we could cover column 2's cells (rows 2 and 4) together with column 3's cells (maybe also rows 2 and 4) using a rectangle spanning columns 2-3 and rows 2-4? But the rectangle must be contiguous rows and columns; rows 2-4 includes row 3, which does not have column 2 or 3 cells. So rectangle would include cells (3,2) and (3,3) which are not in L, violating the condition that tile must be wholly in L. So we cannot include row 3. So rectangle cannot span rows 2-4 unless all rows in between have those columns present. So rectangle cannot skip rows. So the shape's gaps may prevent spanning multiple columns.\n\nThus the lower bound may hold for any permutation because columns have distinct heights and gaps prevent spanning multiple columns without covering cells not in L. But is that always true? Let's analyze more systematically.\n\nWe have shape L defined as set of cells (i,j) with j < c_i. For each